# {{ .Name }}

This service was generated by gox and is maintained with it. Read this file before changing the structure.

## Layout

```
app/                     composition root: one file per concern, Init* functions, app.go knows the order
externalapi/             external APIs this service calls; one folder per provider, interfaces in externalapi.go
internal/
  config/config.go       Config struct; gox appends capability blocks, your fields stay
  domain/                entities per feature; errors.go holds every error with ErrorMap and ErrorTypeMap
  service/               service.go interfaces; one folder per feature
  handler/http/          handler.go interfaces; middleware/, request/, response/; one folder per feature
  storage/               repository.go, cache.go, publisher.go interfaces; one folder per adapter
  testutil/memstore/     in-memory fakes, one file per feature
migrations/              golang-migrate pairs, 000001_name.up.sql and .down.sql
config.yaml              non-secret defaults; secrets come from the environment, see .env.example
gox.yaml                 what gox generates from; edit it, then run gox check
.gox/manifest.json       what gox wrote; do not edit
```

Dependency rule, enforced by `.golangci.yml`: `internal/domain` imports only the standard library; implementations depend on interfaces, never on other implementations; `app` is the only package that knows concrete types.

## Who owns which file

Every generated file is one of three classes, recorded in `.gox/manifest.json`:

- **once**: written one time, then yours. gox never rewrites it. Entities, services, handlers, repositories, tests, migrations, `app/app.go`.
- **tool**: regenerated on every gox run under a `// Code generated by gox. DO NOT EDIT.` header. Do not edit: `app/infra.go`, `app/external_api.go`, `app/storage.go`, `app/cache.go`, `app/service.go`, `app/handler.go`, `app/routes.go`. If you must, run the command again with `--adopt` and the file becomes yours.
- **codemod**: yours, but gox appends and removes its own declarations in exactly seven files: `internal/storage/repository.go`, `internal/storage/cache.go`, `internal/service/service.go`, `internal/handler/handler.go`, `externalapi/externalapi.go`, `internal/domain/errors.go`, `internal/config/config.go`. Add your own declarations freely; gox leaves them alone.

## Startup flow

`cmd/main.go` calls `app.Initialize`. `app/app.go` calls, in order: `InitConfig`, `InitLogger`, `InitInfra` (database, redis, kafka clients), `InitExternalAPI`, `InitStorage`, `InitCache`, `InitService`, `InitHandler`, `InitRoutes`, then serves. Each `Init*` in `app` calls the feature package's `New` and hands the next layer only the interface it needs. Nothing below `app` sees the `Infra`, `Storage`, `Service` or `Handler` structs.

A constructor's signature is its contract with the composition root. Add a parameter to a `New`, for example `cfg config.ServerConfig` or another feature's repository, update the tests that call it, and run `gox sync`: gox reads the signature, picks each argument by type from what the `Init` function has in scope, and regenerates the `app` files. A parameter with no provider, or with more than one, is reported with the candidates.

## Request flow

HTTP request → the router ({{ .HTTP }}) and `internal/handler/http/middleware` → `internal/handler/http/<feature>` (`request.Decode`, which enforces a 1 MiB limit, rejects unknown fields and runs the DTO's `Validate` method) → `internal/service/<feature>` (calls the entity's `New` for invariants, then the repository through `storage.<Feature>Repository`) → `internal/storage/<db>/<feature>` (SQL; errors through `<db>.Translate`) → database. Back up the same way; the handler ends in `response.Success` or `response.Error`. `app` is not on this path, and the interface files are compile-time contracts, not hops.

## Errors

Every error is an `errors.New` sentinel in `internal/domain/errors.go` with one line in `ErrorMap` (status) and one in `ErrorTypeMap` (type). Wrap with `fmt.Errorf("context: %w", err)` or `domain.Wrap(entry, cause)`; `response.Error` resolves through the wrap chain. Anything not in the maps is returned as a fixed 500. Never write JSON by hand in a handler.

## Commands

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Add a feature with every layer and its wiring | `gox gen feature <Name> name:string capacity:int opened_at:time?` |
| Remove a feature | `gox destroy feature <Name>` |
| Add infrastructure | `gox add redis` / `gox add kafka` / `gox remove kafka` |
| See what a command would do | append `--dry-run` |
| Check generated files are current | `gox check` (CI runs it; exit 1 on drift) |
| Re-wire after changing a constructor | `gox sync` (gen and destroy run it too) |
| Diagnose the project | `gox doctor` |
| Customise what gox generates | `gox templates list`, `gox templates eject <id>`, `gox templates diff` |
| Everything else | `make run`, `make test`, `make lint`, `make migrate-up` |

